Property types and investment models differ in leases, operations, development, and risk.|An occupied office and an undeveloped site have different cash-flow timing.|Use identical assumptions for both?|No|Yes|Only in one city
Rental cash depends on occupied space, rent, collections, and operating expenses.|Potential rent ₹100 with 10% vacancy gives ₹90 before other losses.|Collected revenue necessarily equals potential rent?|No|Yes|Only for long leases
NOI deducts property operating costs before financing and defined capital items; cap rate relates NOI to price.|NOI ₹10 at 5% cap rate implies simplified value ₹200.|Higher cap rate with unchanged NOI implies…?|Lower value|Higher value|Unchanged value
Property valuation uses income, comparable sales, and cost approaches with property-specific assumptions.|Two neighbouring buildings have different lease quality and vacancy.|Location alone guarantees equal values?|No|Yes|Only for offices
Development feasibility models land, construction, delays, sales or rents, taxes, and funding.|A six-month delay raises interest and postpones receipts.|Delay affects…?|Both costs and cash timing|Only appearance|Only depreciation
Mortgages may amortise, use interest-only periods, or balloon repayment; payment structure affects risk.|An interest-only loan leaves principal due at maturity.|Paying interest automatically reduces principal?|No|Yes|Only for housing
LTV measures debt/value; debt yield NOI/debt; DSCR cash coverage of debt service.|Debt ₹60 and property value ₹100 give LTV 60%.|A higher appraisal alone improves actual cash debt service?|No|Yes|Only for rentals
Unlevered returns reflect property economics; levered returns include financing flows.|Borrowing amplifies equity gains and losses when asset outcomes change.|Leverage only increases upside?|No|Yes|Only with fixed rates
Property waterfalls distribute cash by contractual priorities, hurdles, and promote terms.|An investor receives preferred distributions before a sponsor's higher share begins.|All partners always receive proportional cash?|No|Yes|Only after construction
Project finance relies primarily on project cash and structures; corporate finance relies on broader company resources.|A project lender analyses contractual revenue and a ring-fenced SPV.|Project lending means no credit risk?|No|Yes|Only with contracts
SPVs isolate activities; limited recourse restricts specified lender remedies under agreed terms.|Lenders may retain construction guarantees despite limited operating recourse.|Limited recourse necessarily means zero sponsor obligations?|No|Yes|Only at financial close
Construction risk includes completion and cost; operating risk includes demand, availability, and maintenance.|Cost overruns occur before a plant starts earning revenue.|This directly creates…?|Extra funding needs|Operating cash surplus|Lower project cost
Concessions and offtake agreements allocate rights and payment risks; PPPs allocate public and private responsibilities.|A power contract specifies availability, price, and buyer obligations.|A contract removes buyer default and enforcement risk?|No|Yes|Only if long term
Debt sizing reflects cash capacity; sculpting shapes repayments to projected available cash.|Lower early cash suggests lower early principal payments, subject to lender rules.|Debt should be sized only from construction cost?|No|Yes|Only for infrastructure
DSCR covers period service; LLCR compares loan-life cash PV with debt; PLCR uses project-life cash PV.|Cash available ₹30 and debt service ₹20 gives DSCR 1.5.|LLCR and DSCR necessarily measure identical horizons?|No|Yes|Only with fixed rates
Reserves and waterfalls prioritise operating costs, debt, required buffers, and owner distributions.|Cash cannot be distributed before required reserve replenishment.|All positive cash is immediately distributable?|No|Yes|Only after operations begin
Refinancing changes debt timing, pricing, and equity proceeds but adds execution risk.|Early cash refinancing improves measured equity IRR while increasing debt.|Higher IRR alone proves better total economics?|No|Yes|Only for property
Infrastructure sectors have distinct demand, tariff, regulation, and availability drivers.|A regulated utility differs from a traffic-sensitive toll road.|One demand forecast fits all infrastructure assets?|No|Yes|Only under PPPs
